From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 08:25:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 59/60] perf(previews): a frame's picture is drawn once its
 design settles, not at the first look

The editor shows a frame that is not a live document as its picture, and a
picture was drawn by Chromium only when first asked for. Whoever first opened
a page after an edit, often an agent's fresh work, looked at white boxes while
the server drew them three at a time (0.3-0.7 s a screen-sized frame, 2-6 s a
tall page). A production log had 149 such picture requests in one minute,
for 58 frames, waiting 38 s at the median and 54 s at worst.

server/prerender.ts draws a frame's picture in the background once its design
has settled:
- the store tells it of a frame whose html, width or height changed (a new
  onDrawnChange hook, after the commit); a move, a rename, a lock or a restack
  is not a change of picture (the previous patch);
- SETTLE_MS (4 s) after the last change, and not while an agent is streaming
  into the frame (every chunk is a change; it looks again a settle later);
- only the smallest level (0.25): it is what every frame at fit and every
  frame away from the view asks for when a page opens. The larger ones stay
  drawn on demand;
- one frame at a time at background priority, outside the per-IP render
  budget (a signed-out visitor's 12 renders a minute were where its boxes
  stayed white), and only after QUIET_MS (2 s) without an interactive render:
  the render gate now counts interactive renders and when one last came or
  went (renderBrowser.ts), since a background page shares the cores with an
  agent's screenshots, and an agent's burst of edits is often followed by its
  own screenshots;
- a frame whose stored picture is already current, a hidden frame, an empty
  one or a canvas element (always live) is skipped.

Measured on a local production build (headless Chrome, software GL, 4 cores,
copies of real pages, median of 3), opening a page as a new visitor 15-25 s
after an agent's edit, all frames in view shown at:
- 10 screens edited on a page of 29 in view: 2.27 -> 1.70 s;
- 2 tall frames edited: 5.99 -> 1.61 s.
Both are what the page takes with nothing edited. A burst of 30 edits costs
24 s of CPU (2.5 s with nothing drawn ahead) and 300 MB more at the peak,
drawn within 35 s; five screenshots taken 5 s into it: 0.87, 0.24, 0.26,
0.33, 0.35 s against 0.57, 0.25, 0.31, 0.31, 0.36 s (before the quiet spell,
0.31-1.33 s).

PREVIEW_PRERENDER=0 turns it off, for a deployment whose renderer bills per
session.
